WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.283
Same world model, same keypress.

00:00:02.283 --> 00:00:05.217
The left one takes over eight hundred milliseconds.

00:00:05.597 --> 00:00:06.943
The right one, about fifty.

00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:11.143
Hi, I'm Shard.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:14.678
Eleven open releases this week, read through their weight headers.

00:00:14.678 --> 00:00:16.092
The loop model first.

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.329
SenseNova Loop nine B stores thirty-two layers.

00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:22.297
The coloured ones are the loop.

00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:27.391
The first four run once, the loop runs twice, the last four once.

00:00:27.391 --> 00:00:29.189
Fifty-six executions from thirty-two layers.

00:00:29.608 --> 00:00:34.304
Each pass carries two residual streams, averaged into one vector between passes.

00:00:34.724 --> 00:00:36.622
The bill is the cache.

00:00:36.622 --> 00:00:40.797
Every execution keeps its own slot: fifty-six kibibytes a token, near

00:00:40.797 --> 00:00:42.316
the twenty-seven B's sixty-four.

00:00:44.158 --> 00:00:45.702
Here is SenseNova's own drawing.

00:00:46.082 --> 00:00:49.887
A router mixes the two streams through a matrix whose rows and

00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:51.155
columns sum to one.

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:52.741
Signals blend, but cannot grow.

00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:56.324
Per token it touches thirteen billion parameters.

00:00:56.324 --> 00:01:00.935
The plain nine B touches eight, the twenty-seven B about twice as many.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.937
The card never names a base.

00:01:03.937 --> 00:01:06.644
Its tensors match Qwen three point five, nine B.

00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:08.449
It is that model, trained further.

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:14.568
Against Qwen three point eight, twenty-seven B: two wins, a tie and two losses.

00:01:14.948 --> 00:01:18.271
The banking loss is the biggest gap, and the card's text skips it.

00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:20.668
Why two passes?

00:01:20.668 --> 00:01:23.069
Most of the gain comes from the first doubling.

00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:26.930
Back to the opening clip.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.724
Apple found the denoiser was the minority of the time.

00:01:30.104 --> 00:01:34.318
The VAE decoder alone took a hundred and forty-seven milliseconds.

00:01:34.698 --> 00:01:38.583
A tiny pixel decoder and a cache-repair network bring a frame to

00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:40.849
about thirty-six, on one B two hundred.

00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:45.728
Hy4 flash ties Claude Haiku in Tencent's blind test, at an

00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:47.056
eighteenth of the cost.

00:01:47.436 --> 00:01:49.276
The row the post left out.

00:01:49.276 --> 00:01:52.035
DeepSeek V four point one Flash beat them both.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.616
Also this week: Inworld's voice isolator.

00:01:55.616 --> 00:01:57.782
The noise goes, the speech stays.

00:01:58.162 --> 00:02:01.775
On my 3090 the offline model cleaned a minute of audio

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:03.089
in under eight seconds.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:05.388
The streaming one fell behind real time.

00:02:06.808 --> 00:02:08.773
Read the headers before the headline.

00:02:08.773 --> 00:02:13.031
Big totals are often lookup tables, and new models are often old ones.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.178
A loop that buys depth with time, a video model with new

00:02:18.178 --> 00:02:20.197
plumbing, and a cheap model that is cheap.

00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:22.664
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:22.664 --> 00:02:23.249
I'm Shard.

00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:23.541
Bye!
